22:03:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There are sometimes reported **average Wonderlic differences by position**, but that doesn’t support “big players are less intelligent.” A few important caveats: - **The Wonderlic is not an IQ test.** It’s a short, timed screening test of basic verbal and quantitative reasoning. It captures some cognitive skills, but not overall intelligence, judgment, creativity, football knowledge, or practical problem-solving. - **The public NFL data are incomplete and often unreliable.** Many scores are leaked, self-reported, misreported, or based on small samples. Broad position-average charts get repeated online as if they were definitive research. - **Quarterbacks do often score relatively high on average**, which makes sense because the position involves processing a playbook, reading defenses, and making rapid decisions. But high scores are neither necessary nor sufficient to be a good quarterback. - **“Receivers score higher than linemen” is not a stable general finding.** In many widely circulated position-average lists, wide receivers and running backs are among the lower-scoring groups, while centers and some offensive linemen score comparatively well. Offensive line play itself involves lots of assignments, calls, recognition of fronts and blitzes, and coordination. - **Even a real group average difference would have huge overlap.** Averages cannot tell you much about any individual player. There are highly academic linemen and less-academic quarterbacks, just as there are in any profession. So the defensible conclusion is that positions can select for different skills and incentives, and football culture can sometimes put less emphasis on academics. But it is not sound to infer intelligence from someone’s body size or call a whole category of people “meatheads” based on that.
